    "media:imagetext": "(Family Letter No. 4)\nTokyo\nTuesday, February 7, 1950\nDear Family,\nOn Saturday, February 4 Amos Hiatt of PAA, who is\nhelping me on the Tokyo deal, and I drove down to Kobe with -\nof all things - two Turks.\nAn uncle and his nephew.\nThe\nformer hes a history which is tragic.\nHe was born in a little\ntown on the Amur River in Manchuria where his father was a\nwealthy merchant dealing in wool and furs. In 1920 the Russian\nReds killed his father.\nThe mother escaped down the Amur in\nsmall boat with her eight children, was picked up at sea by\na Japanese gunboat and brought to Kobe. There the children\ngot along on next to nothing but survived.\nPink the on a back.\nto Manchuria when he was eighteen and worked for his father's\nold firm whichhad been taken over by some Jews.\nLater he went\ninto business for himself out in Sinkiang Province and ran a\nfleet of trucks along the old silk road from Peiping across\nthe Gobi Desert to Hami, Turfan and throughout northwestern\nChina.\nHe made quite a fortune.\nHe had to drop everything\nwhen the Communists took over in China. Now his wife is in\nPeiping while he\nis staying in Kobe with his sister who is\nmarried to another wealthy Turk. It was the sister's son who\ndrove the car - a Plymouth. The boy is very good-looking. He\nhas lived all his nineteen years in Japan - speaks English like\nan American and Japanese like a Japanese.\nWe started out at 5:00 a.m. in the dark, skirting\nYokohama and going on to the Fugia Hotel at Menoshita\nbreakfast. Leaving there we had our first view of Fuji which,\nthrough the morning haze, looked as red as it is depicted on\nthe Japanese painted pillow covers which the sailors buy in\nPanama.\nLater it was gold color.\nWe stopped at Lake Hakone\nwhere our host, the uncle, took some color films which I hope\nto bring home to show you all. We drove all day through little\nJapanese villages and open country over roads sometimes good\nbut often in bad shape.\nOne flat tire tied us up in a little\ntown next to e small sawmill. The owner brought us out three\nchairs and we had lunch - beer, sandwiches and fried chicken\nwhich we had brought along - sitting in the lumber yard with a\nbeautiful view of nearby Fuji with a warm sun shining out of\na cloudless sky. We drove on through Nagoya, Kyoto and Osaka\nall big cities - to Kobe where we arrived at 10:30 p.m. some\n400 miles from Tokyo.\nOur host's sister and husband have a large German-\nstyle house on a hillside looking out over the city and bay.\nThe house is surrounded by a lovely Japanese garden.\nThe owner,\nF. Gizatullin, (pronounced like it is spelled) and his wife\ncouldn't have been more cordial or hospitable.\nThey have five\nother children besides Frederick (our chauffeur), three girls\nand two boys. The children all speak English, Turkish and\nJapanese with equal facility but the father's English was not",
